The polls had consensus in favor, and the paper will be forwarded to LWG for C++29.

# Selected Poll Comments # {#poll-comments}

For some of the comments, small parts were removed to anonymize.


## Poll 1: Send "P0870R8: A proposal for a type trait to detect narrowing conversions" (with added reflection type trait) back to LWG for C++29.

> This is essential to improving casting and where casting might be used in templates.
>
> — Strongly Favor

> This is incredibly useful. I've often wanted this facility. It's error-prone to write by hand.
>
> — Strongly Favor

> The functionality provided by this is useful.
>
> — Weakly Favor


> This may change it meaning over time, but otherwise usefull.
>
> — Weakly Favor


## Poll 2: Modify "P2249R6: Mixed comparisons for smart pointers" with the action items described below, and forward to LWG for C++29.

Action items:

* 6.2.2 typo ‘<’ should be ‘>’ at end of compare_three_way_result_t line. Same line 2 below.
* Ensure that the wording reflects the requirements under [hidden.friends] as the intent is for these comparisons to be hidden friends.
* Ensure shared_ptr comparisons are constexpr (LWG/author should rebase on P3037).

> This is essential to making smart pointers easier to use.
>
> — Strongly Favor

> Very reasonable addition, but we need object_ptr / observer_ptr to fix the remaining issues highlighted by the paper (common_reference_type between pointers and smart pointers).
>
> — Strongly Favor

> This facility helps users avoid getting raw pointers when they could just work directly with the smart pointers in question.
>
> — Strongly Favor

> The fewer .get() calls the better.
>
> — Strongly Favor

> Seem usable, low implementation cost.
>
> — Weakly Favor


## Poll 3: Apply naming changes ("notify_is.." into "is_notify" (etc.), and the FTM) and forward the updated P3255R3 to LWG for C++29 and as a DR up to C++20 (section 10 wording for DR, section 11 wording for C++29).

> This is good metadata that can influence implementation choices.
>
> — Strongly Favor

> This is a useful facility that improves portability.  I have use cases in mind already.
>
> — Strongly Favor

> This closes a gap in the standard library interface for atomics and is highly useful.
>
> — Strongly Favor

> Feels a little bit rushed because we did bring up a lot of issues (free functions, constexpr atomics, etc.) up during telecon that have not been discussed before, but all of those seem to be addressed by the paper properly.
>
> — Weakly Favor

> Seems like a decent thing to do. Allowing reasonable implementations is a good part of this paper. I think the paper should do a better job at showing what users are expected to do with different results of these functions, tho.
>
> — Weakly Favor

> There is a great risk of user assuming that lock-free on wait function would imply Address-free. If we want to guaranteed signal safety, we should simply do so.
>
> — Weakly Against



## Poll 4: Apply changes (apply section 8 wording option A, the rename of the member functions from "high_bits" / "low_bits" into "high" and "low", and remove the "3 recommended practice") and re-send the updated P3642R7 paper to LWG for C++29.

> When will we have _umul128?
>
> — Strongly Favor

> This is essential to some safer math operations. We take our wins, wherever we can find them.
>
> — Strongly Favor

> I appreciate the addition of low-level arithmetic facilities like this. They address niche use cases, but solving those use cases without special support is not possible.
>
> — Strongly Favor

> This is a useful facility.  The naming changes are good.
>
> — Strongly Favor

> Having members would allow me to store wider type, and mask it on access.
>
> — Weakly Favor

> The changes to apply make it better; they are too new to be strongly in favor of this paper.
>
> — Weakly Favor

> The renaming seem to decrease clarity. If people don't like the "bit" part, the name should at least indicate what it is.
>
> — Weakly Against



